== 3.18 Multi-Tier Cross-Modality Response Prediction Across 13 References and 15 Bulk Cohorts
While self-reference pseudobulk validation (Section 3.12) and first-principles synthetic benchmarks (Sections 3.14–3.17) establish the theoretical operating regimes of bulk deconvolution, clinical translation requires determining whether fine-grained cell-state atlases derived from independent single-cell cohorts predict checkpoint blockade outcomes in unfractionated clinical trial bulk RNA-seq. Using the multi-stage pipeline orchestrated via `tme_datasets` (Section 2.13, @fig-tme-datasets-pipeline-dag), we deconvolved 11 clinical bulk RNA-seq trials and 4 indication/pan-cancer pooled strata ($N = 1,058$ evaluable pre-treatment patients; 9 CRI iAtlas trials totaling $N = 1,015$ plus 2 anti-PD-(L)1 NSCLC cohorts totaling $N = 43$) against all 13 hierarchical single-cell references ($3.60 times 10^6$ cells, 355 Leiden $r = 2.0$ states across Tiers 1A, 1B, 2, and 3; Section 2.14) and evaluated 5 supervised classifier families under $5 times 5$ repeated stratified cross-validation (@fig-iatlas-roc-auc, @fig-iatlas-prauc).

