  placement: auto,
  image("../figures/milo-deconv/iatlas_nonlinear_conditional_interactions.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    *Focused non-linear conditional interactions ($8 times 8$) and stromal exclusion gating of clinical ICB response ($N = 1,015$).*
    *(a)* Symmetric $8 times 8$ pairwise interaction matrix across the 6 positive-residual RF outlier states plus 2 exhaustion/myeloid anchors (`M25: CTLA4+TIGIT+ Tex`, `M32: MRC1+FABP4+ M2-TAM`), encoding signed Random Forest 2D partial-dependence interaction strength and trial-adjusted logistic epistasis Wald $z_(i j)$, resolving a clear block split between *Lymphoid–Humoral Synergy* (`+/+`, Okabe-Ito Blue) and *Stromal–Myeloid Gating* (`+/−`, Vermilion).
    *(b)* Empirical $3 times 3$ tertile-stratified clinical responder rates ($N = 1,015$, Wilson 95% CIs) across focused Lymphoid–Humoral infiltration tertiles (`T1`–`T3`, `M04–M30`) conditioned on `myCAF/M2` stromal barrier tertiles: in *Permissive Low-Stroma (`T1`)* tumors, responder rates surge from $26.9%$ ($n = 145$) to $32.5%$ ($n = 77$) to *$61.5%$* ($n = 117$, conditional $r = +0.286$), whereas in *Excluded High-Stroma (`T3`)* tumors, responder rates remain completely blunted at $17.8% -> 24.8% -> 20.7%$ ($r = +0.002$).
  ]
) <fig-nonlinear-interactions-8>

1. *Supra-Additive Lymphoid–Humoral & Chemokine Co-Activation Synergy ($z_(i j) > +3.0$)*:
   Within the focused $8 times 8$ submatrix (@fig-nonlinear-interactions-8 a), co-infiltration of humoral and cytotoxic effectors produced potent positive epistasis: `M29 (JCHAIN+ Plasma)` $times$ `M30 (MS4A1+ TLS B)` ($z = +3.57, H = 0.128$), `M04 (GNLY+ Eff. T)` $times$ `M30 (TLS B)` ($z = +3.48, H = 0.176$), and `M04 (GNLY+ Eff. T)` $times$ `M29 (Plasma)` ($z = +3.44, H = 0.068$). Expanding to all $binom(42, 2) = 861$ pan-atlas pairs (@fig-nonlinear-interactions-all42 a, b) revealed that this synergy generalizes across follicular helper/resident T cells, exhausted neoantigen-reactive CD8 T cells, interferon-primed macrophages, and innate effectors—led by `M13 (CXCL13+PDCD1+ Tex)` $times$ `M24 (CXCL13+NMB+ Tfh/Trm)` ($z = +3.74, H = 0.250$), `M08` $times$ `M09` ($z = +3.71, H = 0.239$), `M09` $times$ `M24` ($z = +3.70, H = 0.199$), `M29 (Plasma)` $times$ `M36 (FCGR3A+GNLY+ NK)` ($z = +3.49, H = 0.091$, consistent with IgG1-mediated antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity within mature TLS), `M14 (CXCL9+STAT1+ M1-TAM)` $times$ `M29 (Plasma)` ($z = +3.46$), and `M29 (Plasma)` $times$ `M34 (GZMK+ Tem CD8+)` ($z = +3.46$). Across all 41 partner states (@fig-nonlinear-interactions-all42 b), *`M29: JCHAIN+MZB1+ Plasma`* ranked as the `#1` global epistatic hub in the human TME ($overline(|z|) = 2.67$, mean $H = 0.076$, $|hat(beta)_("meta")| = 0.81$), followed closely by `M09: SIGLEC8+CR1+ Granul.` ($overline(|z|) = 2.63, overline(H) = 0.099$), `M14: CXCL9+STAT1+ M1-TAM` ($overline(|z|) = 2.61$), `M13: CXCL13+PDCD1+ Tex` ($overline(|z|) = 2.59$), `M34: GZMK+NKG7+ Tem CD8+` ($overline(|z|) = 2.56$), and `M04: GNLY+PRF1+ Eff. T` ($overline(|z|) = 2.52$).

2. *Antagonistic Fibrotic/Myeloid Exclusion Gating ($z_(i j) < -3.0$) & Complete Blunting of Responder Gradients*:
   In the off-diagonal quadrants of both interaction matrices (@fig-nonlinear-interactions-8 a, @fig-nonlinear-interactions-all42 a), myofibroblastic CAFs (`M17`), hypoxic parenchyma (`M12`), and terminal exhaustion/suppressive niches exerted strong negative epistatic gating on lymphoid and humoral effectors: `M05 (TRAF3+CD96+ T)` $times$ `M17 (ISLR+COL5A2+ myCAF)` ($z = -3.62, H = 0.129$), `M17 (myCAF)` $times$ `M30 (MS4A1+ TLS B)` ($z = -3.42, H = 0.109$), `M04 (GNLY+ Eff. T)` $times$ `M17 (myCAF)` ($z = -3.29, H = 0.064$), `M25 (CTLA4+TIGIT+ Tex)` $times$ `M29 (Plasma)` ($z = -3.33, H = 0.131$), and `M04 (GNLY+ Eff. T)` $times$ `M12 (CA9+SLC2A1+ Hypox.)` ($z = -3.19, H = 0.110$). Biologically, dense `ISLR+COL5A2+` collagen bundles and hypoxic metabolic barriers physically sequester T and B lymphocytes in peritumoral stroma (`immune-excluded` architecture), preventing bulk mRNA transcripts from translating into juxtacrine tumor killing.

